<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Maynard Keynes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Maynard Keynes was an English economist whose General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936) argued that governments should manage demand to fight unemployment, founding Keynesian economics. He had criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> in The Economic Consequences of the Peace (1919), and he led the British delegation at the Bretton Woods Conference of 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-maynard-keynes.jpg" alt="John Maynard Keynes" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, dedicated to Bettmann Archive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Maynard_Keynes_(1929).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As a permanent member of the United Nations Security Council, a founder of NATO and a nuclear power, it shaped the post-war order, and <b>John Maynard Keynes</b> helped design the Bretton Woods system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Jean de Sismondi’s New Principles of Political Economy (1819) was the first comprehensive liberal critique of early capitalism, and <b>John Maynard Keynes</b> rejected the classical belief that an unfettered market reaches full employment on its own.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Maynard Keynes</b> judged its attention to production and to bullion rational for its time, and Paul Samuelson wrote that with employment less than full “all the debunked mercantilist arguments turn out to be valid”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Firle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.21,0.12&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Cambridge on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4553 361 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Maynard Keynes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5003 631h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5003 631h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.21,0.12&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
